  {
   "transparent": false,
   "type": "text",
   "title": "A BMC firmware update on this lab, checked against Redfish",
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "content": "Each fact is checked twice: against the latest DMTF Redfish schemas, and on a lab BMC (bmc10, 6 Oct 2026), by requests built as `rollout.py` builds them. Every pipeline's own evidence is below.\n\n| | The Redfish standard | This lab's BMC: OpenBMC GB200 NVL, bmcweb | What the rollout does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1 · `@Redfish.OperationApplyTime` is `OnReset`** | `Settings.OperationApplyTime` (Settings v1_5_0): `Immediate`, `OnReset` (*applied when the system or service is reset*), `AtMaintenanceWindowStart`, `InMaintenanceWindowOnReset`, `OnStartUpdateRequest`, `OnTargetReset`. A multipart push carries it in its `UpdateParameters` part. A service may advertise the values it takes in `@Redfish.OperationApplyTimeSupport` | UpdateService v1_11_1 advertises none, so each value was tried: **`OnReset` → 202** and a Task; `OnTargetReset`, `AtMaintenanceWindowStart`, `InMaintenanceWindowOnReset`, `OnStartUpdateRequest` → **400 `PropertyValueNotInList`**. `Immediate` is taken too, and flashes and reboots inside the Task | sends `OnReset` on every update, so the Task's end is seen before the BMC goes away, and the rollout chooses the reset and times the return ✓ |\n| **2 · `OnReset` resets the BMC only: the host isn't restarted** | `OnReset` applies the update when *the system or service* is reset. A BMC image's `SoftwareInventory.RelatedItem` is its Manager, so the reset that applies it is `Manager.Reset`; only a `ComputerSystem.Reset` or `Chassis.Reset` restarts the host | `RelatedItem` → `/redfish/v1/Managers/bmc`; `Manager.Reset` takes `GracefulRestart` and `ForceRestart`. After a **Completed** `OnReset` update the BMC **still ran the old version**; the **Manager reset** (back in 215 s) booted the new one. No `ComputerSystem.Reset` was sent | resets the Manager, `GracefulRestart`, then post-checks; the drain is skipped: *not needed: the reset leaves the host running* ✓ |\n| **3 · A Task tracks the update; it isn't promoted to a Job** | The push answers 202 Accepted with a Task (TaskService). A service that schedules the work may create a Job (JobService: `JobState`, `Schedule`) and list it in the Task's `Links.CreatedResources` | Task v1_4_3 at `/redfish/v1/TaskService/Tasks/0`: Running → **Completed**, 100 %, `TaskStarted`, `TaskProgressChanged`, `TaskCompletedOK`. **No `Links`**: nothing created. **No JobService**: `GET /redfish/v1/JobService` → 404 | follows the Task's `TaskState`, `PercentComplete` and `Messages`, and records each change; pre-flight and the post-check read Jobs too, where a BMC has them ✓ |\n| **4 · The image is never Staged or Armed, so there is nothing to cancel** | SoftwareInventory v1_12: `Active`, `Staged` (*queued for activation on the next `Activate` action*); v1_15: `Armed` (*will become active on the next reset*), `ImageState`; v1_14: the `Cancel` action *cancels the pending activation*, and errors if there is none | SoftwareInventory **v1_1_0**: none of these, no `Activate`, no `Cancel`. The running image is named by `Manager.Links.ActiveSoftwareImage`, the only one in `SoftwareImages`. While a Completed `OnReset` update waited for its reset, the BMC reported **no pending image**: the firmware inventory was empty until the reset. After a **failed** update (`TaskAborted`), the inventory was unchanged and a reset **booted the same version**: nothing was pending | never stages: `OnReset`, then its own reset at once. A failure before the reset leaves the old firmware running and nothing to cancel; after the reset, the rollback reinstalls the version before ✓ |\n"
   },
   "id": 1,
   "gridPos": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 0,
    "w": 24,
    "h": 17
   }
  },
  {
   "transparent": false,
   "type": "text",
   "title": "Rollback, per resource the rollout updates",
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "content": "Only a way back the rollout carries out counts: a host without one is blocked in pre-flight unless `--accept-no-rollback`. The plan shows each host's below.\n\n| Resource the image updates | How the update activates | The host | Rollback the rollout carries out | Not built |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Manager**: BMC firmware, the lab's only updatable component | `Manager.Reset`, `GracefulRestart` before `ForceRestart`, from its `ResetActionInfo` | keeps running: not drained | reinstall the version that ran before, from the catalog's package, the same way (push or pull, `OnReset`, `Manager.Reset`), then the post-check again | switching to an A/B bank (`Manager.Links.ActiveSoftwareImage`, `SoftwareInventory.Activate`): a bank the BMC reports doesn't count as a way back |\n| **ComputerSystem**: BIOS, host firmware | `ComputerSystem.Reset`, `GracefulRestart`, `ForceRestart`, then `PowerCycle` | restarts: drained first, given back after | reinstall, `ComputerSystem.Reset`, post-check, then undrain | the same bank switch |\n| **Chassis**: a device whose `RelatedItem` is a Chassis | `Chassis.Reset`, `PowerCycle` first | restarts: drained first | reinstall, `Chassis.Reset`, post-check, undrain | the same bank switch |\n| Any component with `ResetRequiredOnUpdate: false` | no reset | keeps running | reinstall, no reset | |\n\n**When it fails.** Before the reset (a Task that ends in `Exception`, a rejected image): nothing to roll back, the old firmware still runs, and nothing waits to apply on a later reset. After the reset (a post-check that fails, a BMC that doesn't come back): the rollback reinstalls the version before. A rollback that fails too, or no way back: `needs_attention`, left drained for a person. An undrain that fails: `needs_attention`.\n"
   },
   "id": 2,
   "gridPos": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 17,
    "w": 24,
    "h": 10
   }
